std::vector<const char*> LessonDetails(int id) {
    switch(id) {
    case 2: return {"Addresses and objects","A pointer is itself an object. Its value can identify another object, be null, or be unusable after the target's lifetime. The pointer and the target have separate storage.","Trace the alias","Start at 15. Store &score in p. Read *p into a separate int, then write 20 through p. Predict which of the two integers changes and check in Pointer alias.","Try it","Write a function taking int* that adds four to the caller's integer. Pass &score and check the result in Pointer update."};
    case 8: return {"Array layout","The three array elements are contiguous, but the array does not store its length in each element. Pass the length separately when working with a pointer to the first element.","Trace the loop","For {5, 2, 8}, initialize the minimum to 5. At the second element replace it with 2. At the third element keep 2. Starting with zero would fail on this input.","Try it","Open Array minimum and implement the loop starting at index 1. Include an all-negative test and a repeated minimum."};
    case 10: return {"Storage durations","A local pointer can have automatic storage duration while the allocated object has dynamic storage duration. Leaving the block destroys the pointer. It does not free an allocation that the pointer refers to.","Trace the lifetime","Check the result of malloc, then initialize the object before reading it. Free the original allocation once. Clearing one pointer does not clear other aliases.","Try it","In Heap lifetime, step forward to deallocation and identify which object ends first. Compare the C lesson's malloc/free pair with the lab's C++ new/delete pair."};
    case 12: return {"Calls and base cases","A recursive function needs a case that returns without calling itself. Each recursive call must move toward that case. For factorial on nonnegative inputs, n <= 1 returns 1.","Trace the return","factorial(4) waits for factorial(3), which waits for factorial(2). The call for 1 returns 1. The waiting multiplications produce 2, 6, then 24.","Try it","Open Recursive calls and pause at the base case. Predict the next return before pressing Next. Restrict inputs when using int because large factorials overflow."};
    case 16: return {"Object invariants","A class can keep its representation private and expose operations that preserve valid state. The constructor establishes the starting state, and public methods make permitted changes.","Trace two objects","Create counters a and b. Call a.tick() twice and b.tick() once. Because count is a nonstatic data member, the two objects report 2 and 1 respectively.","Try it","Add a reset() member that restores zero. Keep count private and use the const value() member to check the result."};
    default: return {};
    }
}
